sec.p:
This procedure yielded two estimates of ice loss: 1. Using the anomaly-derived perturbation, the resulting ice loss was 4.1 m SLE, and 2. using the absolute value of elevation, the resulting ice loss was 7.1 m SLE. For each of these values of total ice loss, we also perform a separate perturbation using the equivalent volume of freshwater forcing, and a combined ice loss and freshwater forcing experiment, as follows:
SL4.1: Removal of 4.1 m sea level-equivalent ice sheet using the RCP4.5 - 1900 CE anomaly from Golledge et al.27 (Fig. 1a, c);
FW4.1: Freshwater forcing over 500 years adjacent to the Antarctic coast, with total volume equivalent to the ice loss derived in SL4.1;
COMB4.1: Combined perturbations of ice sheet removal (SL4.1), and freshwater forcing (FW4.1).
SL7.1: Removal of 7.1 m sea level-equivalent ice sheet using the RCP4.5 absolute ice elevation from Golledge et al.27 (Fig. 1b, d);
FW7.1: Freshwater forcing over 500 years adjacent to the Antarctic coast, with total volume equivalent to the ice loss derived in SL7.1;
COMB7.1: Combined perturbations of ice sheet removal (SL7.1), and freshwater forcing (FW7.1).
